cc: keep capability-implied captures on SAM anon-class type members

Under capture checking, a SAM literal whose target is an ordinary trait (not
a `FunctionN`/`js.Function`) is expanded to an anonymous class by `ExpandSAMs`,
which runs *before* `cc.Setup`/`cc.CheckCaptures`. On the JVM such a SAM stays a
closure typed verbatim as the expected refinement, so it is never re-checked
member-by-member. Under `-scalajs`, `SJSPlatform.isSam` rejects arbitrary traits,
so the same SAM becomes an anonymous class ahead of capture checking, and its
synthesised type members are then checked against the declared SAM refinement.

Two things went wrong for a member `type Self = C` whose right-hand side `C`
derives from `Capability` (so its declared spelling is boxed to `C^{any}` by
`transformExplicitType`):

1. `transformExplicitType` stripped the capability-implied capture from the
   member's own info (the `sym.isType` branch), leaving it bare `C`. That is the
   canonical spelling of a standalone `type X = C`, but here it breaks internal
   consistency of the anon class: its abstract method's parameter, typed `C^`,
   no longer conforms to the inherited `stream(value: Self)` seen through the
   bare member. We now keep the implied set for a synthetic type member of an
   anonymous class.

2. Even with both spellings boxed, the two universal roots differ — `caps.any`
   on the freshly synthesised member vs the declared method's `ResultCap`. For a
   capability type the capture is implied and not independently meaningful, so
   `hasMatchingMember` now treats two type-alias members whose right-hand sides
   both derive from `Capability`, and whose capture sets are empty or purely
   root capabilities, as matching when their capture-stripped forms are equal.

The `hasMatchingMember` relaxation only adds an acceptance branch, so it cannot
turn a previously-accepted program into a rejected one; both changes are inert
on the JVM, where the SAM stays a closure with no separate member symbols.

